  "headline": "Dan Birkinshaw storms GW6 as Louis Grimoldby clings to a five-point lead",
  "summary": "Dan Birkinshaw’s 87-point masterclass was the week’s top score, while olly barnes fired 82 to climb into the top four. Louis Grimoldby remains top on 438, but Tim Chodha is only five points back and Mark Horton sits a single point behind him: the title race is already ferocious.",
  "report": "Dan Birkinshaw was the undisputed GW6 headline act. MadjoKarp posted a magnificent 87 points with no hits, comfortably claiming Manager of the Week and powering Dan seven places up the standings. Only olly barnes could get close with 82 for Grasshoppers, while Will Finney’s 79 completed the gameweek podium.\n\nAt the summit, Louis Grimoldby stays put at the top with Grim FC on 438 after a 64-point week, but there is absolutely no room for comfort. Tim Chodha’s Hakuna Juan Mata scored 75 on a wildcard to reach 433, just five behind. Mark Horton delivered 74 for Vespa Mo Pedro and is now on 432, a single point behind Tim, while olly’s 82-point charge takes Grasshoppers to 426, six behind Mark. Four managers, 12 points: with 32 gameweeks remaining, this is already getting properly spicy.\n\nTim’s wildcard was the strongest of a busy chip week, with Mike Flynn’s wildcard returning 71 for Bob Z's Drop Dodgers. Alan Eckett-Brown’s wildcard brought 54, while Chris Beeden and James Geary each scored 46 after playing theirs. The free-hit contingent had a more mixed time: Jamie Mitchell recorded 47, Phil Johnson 42 and Hayley Phelan 36. Chris will be particularly haunted by a colossal 34 bench points — an FPL bench capable of winning a small argument all by itself.\n\nCaptaincy brought both delight and despair. Haaland was the popular armband choice for 10 managers, but his 6 points was hardly a season-defining explosion. Will Finney found the real gold, captaining De Cuyper for 15 points with the multiplier of 2. At the other end, Jamie Mitchell handed the armband to Hall, who returned 0. Hall was owned by 11 managers, so plenty felt the pain; Jamie simply made it official.\n\nThe differential hunters had their moments. Dewsbury-Hall's 13 points rewarded James Geary and James Marshall, while Louis Grimoldby’s solo Yarmoliuk pick returned 8. Vuskovic scored 8 for George Foy and Phil Johnson, Rice collected 7 for Abi Speight and Hayley Phelan, and David Bailey’s lone Ajer selection delivered 6. Louis also had Barry’s 6, shared only with Will Finney — useful little margins when the lead is five points.\n\nFurther down, Ali Smith endured a brutal week. Bamba No. 8 finished with the wooden spoon on 31 net points after a 4-point hit, dropping eight places, the largest fall of GW6. Paul Alexis also took a 4-point hit but still emerged with 63 net points, while Phil Johnson and Callum Bowness each slipped three places after scores of 42 and 44 respectively.\n\nMark Horton remains the league’s hottest manager over the last three gameweeks with 227 net points, and his GW2 score of 116 still stands as the season record. Paul Ray has the coldest three-game stretch at 140, but there is plenty of runway left. For now, though, the top is a five-point squeeze, Dan Birkinshaw owns GW6, and nobody should be taking their captaincy decisions lightly after this one.",
